 Consider a service that records unconditional device updates. Ingestion is its main job. Latest-value lookups happen less often and can tolerate some delay, as long as the service reports it. The lookup table is a projection: a representation derived from the journal to serve those reads. For this design, accepting a write means appending its records to a local journal and successfully synchronizing the file. Only then may the background worker consume them. The reply confirms that the journal has recorded the write; the projection may still be behind. The writer publishes its progress after synchronization:  append bytes  →  synchronize file  →  publish completed position
 These excerpts show that ordering for synchronized writes with a background projection. They omit setup and instrumentation. The Rust writer here is a file; a user-space buffering wrapper would have to be flushed before synchronizing the underlying file.,  Publishing after synchronization: Go, with explicit error propagation. if err = writeAll(f, buffer[:count*width]); err != nil {
    return
}
if err = f.Sync(); err != nil {
    return
}

p.mu.Lock()
p.head = base + count
p.cv.Broadcast()
p.mu.Unlock()
 Publishing after synchronization: Rust, with error propagation through ?. writer.write_all(&buffer[..count * WIDTH])?;
writer.sync_all()?;

{
    let mut state = p.mu.lock()
        .expect("projection lock poisoned");
    state.head = base + count;
    p.cv.notify_all();
}
 Neither version advances head if synchronization reports an error. The mutex makes the new position visible to the worker; file synchronization handles a separate obligation to storage., ,  Rust's write_all keeps writing until the buffer is consumed or it encounters an error it cannot retry. The companion's Go helper loops over partial progress and rejects zero progress. Neither operation makes a batch atomic: an error can leave part of it in the file. Both programs stop on a write or synchronization error instead of retrying an uncertain append.,  Creating the journal is part of the persistence protocol too. Both programs create a new file exclusively and, in synchronized mode, synchronize the empty file and its containing directory before timing writes. On Linux, file synchronization alone does not necessarily persist the directory entry. These setup steps still do not qualify the programs for every power-loss scenario. A crash after synchronization but before the reply can also leave a recorded write that the client believes failed. Retrying safely requires a request identity or an application rule for duplicate updates. Delaying the projection does not resolve this acknowledgement ambiguity. The lookup projection is optional for accepting these unconditional updates. If acceptance depends on a uniqueness check or a conditional update, the service needs sufficiently current state to make that decision before accepting the write.

 Suppose the journal is published through record 10,000 and the projection has applied through 9,700. The view is missing 300 events. These are illustrative positions, not benchmark results.  <span class="figure-number">Figure 2: </span>The view covers a journal prefix; a device's last-update position may be older. Positions and widths are illustrative. A live freshness-wait API is not implemented by the benchmark. Open the full-size diagram A value alone hides that gap. A value accompanied by as_of_sequence = 9,700 states the view's boundary. The value's own last-update sequence may be older; a particular device need not change in every batch. The view must represent a contiguous prefix:  0 <= indexed <= head
projection = apply every record from 1 through indexed, in order
 A single worker reads and validates the next batch outside the lock. It then applies the table changes and advances its position in the same critical section. Readers must never observe the new position with the old table. Updating data and progress together: Go fields protected by convention. p.mu.Lock()
for _, r := range decoded[:count] {
    p.entries[r.Key] = Entry{r.Seq, r.Value}
}
p.indexed = start + count
p.cv.Broadcast()
p.mu.Unlock()
 Updating data and progress together: Rust state accessed through a guard. {
    let mut state = self.mu.lock()
        .expect("projection lock poisoned");
    for r in &decoded[..count] {
        state.entries[r.key as usize] = Entry {
            seq: r.seq,
            value: r.value,
        };
    }
    state.indexed = start + count;
    self.cv.notify_all();
}
 A live reader must copy the entry and the projection position under that same lock. For read-your-writes, the write response can return its journal sequence. A reader waits for indexed > required_sequence= and copies the result while still holding the lock. It may see a later update to the same device: the position is a minimum freshness requirement, not an exact historical snapshot. A timeout says the view did not reach that position before the deadline. The recorded write may still be waiting to appear. Here, “latest” means last in journal order. A delayed device observation appended later will replace an earlier entry, even if its measurement timestamp is older. A service that needs the newest measurement instead needs an event-time or version comparison and a rule for ties. Go places a mutex beside the protected fields. The compiler does not require callers to acquire it. Package boundaries, review, and race-enabled tests help enforce the convention; a race detector only observes executed paths. Rust's Mutex<State> supplies protected access through a guard, whose scope makes the lock lifetime visible. Arc<Projection> lets threads share ownership; the projection still needs synchronization for its mutable state.,  Rust will still compile code that advances indexed too early while holding the correct lock. Preserving the contiguous prefix is our responsibility in either language. Reading and decoding outside the lock also matters because the critical section blocks readers and the writer's progress publication. Parallel replay workers would need an ordered commit protocol: a later batch finishing first must not advance the position past an unfinished earlier batch. The live freshness API also needs its own tests; queries made only after catch-up cannot exercise it.

 A worker can finish a short test after traffic stops and still be too slow for the service's normal arrival rate. Assumed input rate Assumed worker rate Backlog added in 10 seconds 300,000 records/s 270,000 records/s 300,000 records Illustration, not measurement: 30,000 additional records per second × 10 seconds. At 32 bytes per record, the outstanding journal suffix is 9.6 MB. A larger queue gives the worker longer to absorb a burst. If that rate difference persists, the dashboard falls further behind and shutdown has more work to drain. The writer can limit that lag by checking capacity before appending another batch:  current lag + next batch <= lag limit
head - indexed + count <= limit
 When the condition fails, the writer waits. Once the budget fills, a sustained write rate is constrained by the worker's progress. Measuring only the earlier fast acknowledgements would hide that limit.  <span class="figure-number">Figure 3: </span>A record budget makes the writer wait for replay instead of admitting an ever-growing gap. These trajectories are schematic. The bound is on record count, not age, journal size or upstream queues. Open the full-size diagram Waiting for capacity: Go's condition variable has a locker. func (p *projection) reserve(count, limit int) error {
    p.mu.Lock()
    defer p.mu.Unlock()
    for p.head-p.indexed+count > limit && p.failure == nil {
        p.cv.Wait()
    }
    return p.failure
}
 Waiting for capacity: Rust's wait consumes and returns the guard. fn reserve(&self, count: usize, limit: usize) -> io::Result<()> {
    let mut state = self.mu.lock()
        .expect("projection lock poisoned");
    while state.head - state.indexed + count > limit
        && state.failure.is_none()
    {
        state = self.cv.wait(state)
            .expect("projection lock poisoned");
    }
    match &state.failure {
        Some(error) => Err(invalid(error.clone())),
        None => Ok(()),
    }
}
 Both waits release the mutex and reacquire it before continuing. Both loops must recheck the predicate. Go's wait requires notification to return, but notification does not imply the predicate is true. Rust additionally permits spurious wakeups.,  The full worker code records I/O or decoding failures under the lock and notifies waiters. Neither wait has a request deadline or cancellation path, though. Storage that stalls without returning an error can leave callers waiting indefinitely. The full programs reject a lag limit smaller than the configured batch size and cap record counts and batch sizes for their 64-bit target. Those checks matter if this excerpt is reused: an oversized batch can never fit, and unbounded position arithmetic can overflow.,  These excerpts assume one writer. Despite its name, the method checks capacity without reserving it. Two writers could both pass the check and then exceed the limit. Multiple writers need reservations or serialized admission, whichever language implements them. The limit bounds how many accepted records are waiting for replay, not how long they have waited. Even one event can remain stale indefinitely if the worker stops. It also leaves other budgets untouched: the full journal can keep growing and requests may queue upstream. Variable-size or variable-cost events may need a byte or work budget instead of a record count.

 In the replay design, the worker owns a read descriptor and reusable buffers. The writer publishes a position and has no batch memory to share with it. This keeps ownership simple, at the cost of replay and decoding. An alternative sends the decoded batch directly after persistence. This avoids replay, but the writer must not reuse the batch while the worker is still reading it. The next two functions show different handoff contracts. Go keeps the caller's original buffer; Rust transfers it. These are teaching examples from examples/handoff, separate from the journal policies being compared. Go: keep the original and copy the records for the worker. func EnqueueCopy(
    ctx context.Context,
    out chan<- []Record,
    batch []Record,
) error {
    owned := append([]Record(nil), batch...)
    select {
    case out <- owned:
        return nil
    case <-ctx.Done():
        return ctx.Err()
    }
}
 Rust: move the existing allocation to the worker. pub fn enqueue_owned(
    out: &SyncSender<Vec<Record>>,
    batch: Vec<Record>,
) -> Result<(), SendError<Vec<Record>>> {
    out.send(batch)
}

 A Go channel transfers the slice descriptor, not an independent backing array. Sending it does not prohibit later access by the sender, nor establish that processing is finished. This version copies the batch to preserve the caller's ability to reuse the original after the call.,  Rust moves the vector into the channel. The caller cannot keep using the moved value, and the backing array is not cloned. If the receiver disconnects before the send succeeds, SendError carries the unsent vector back., ,   <span class="figure-number">Figure 4: </span>These teaching functions have different caller contracts: Go retains an original and sends a copy; Rust transfers the existing allocation. Both need a return protocol to reuse storage. Sending is not processing completion. Open the full-size diagram Successful submission still does not mean the worker has applied the batch. A buffered send can finish before the receiver takes it; even an unbuffered handoff only confirms receipt. The projection's progress position remains the way to tell whether processing has finished.,  Choice Go Rust Transfer without copying payload Possible; sender relinquishes access by convention Ownership move restricts subsequent use Retain an independently mutable original Copy the records Clone or otherwise copy the records Reuse storage after worker completion Return buffer through an agreed protocol Return the owned vector through a protocol Waiting in the shown API Context can cancel the wait Standard send waits for capacity or disconnection Moving a reusable buffer transfers its allocation too:  let submitted = std::mem::take(&mut batch);
enqueue_owned(&tx, submitted)?;
// `batch` is now empty; its former allocation moved to the worker.
 The writer therefore needs replacement storage for its next batch. A pool with a return path can reuse allocations in either language. Go relies on a convention that the sender stops accessing transferred storage; Rust checks the move at compile time. These records contain only integers, so the Go slice copy duplicates all record data. With pointers or nested slices it would be shallow. The caller must keep the input stable while copying, and channel closure must be coordinated with sends. The Go function copies before it checks cancellation. If both the send and cancellation are ready, the select may choose either, so cancellation has no priority. Rust's standard SyncSender::send takes no cancellation token at all; a deadline-aware submission protocol needs additional machinery or another channel API.,  A bounded channel limits queued messages, but callers can still allocate batches before blocking on it. In this Go example, each waiting caller retains an extra copy. Bound the number of producers and batch sizes, or acquire capacity before allocating, if the channel is meant to enforce a memory budget. The same issue applies to Rust producers retaining owned vectors while waiting to send. Direct handoff would still need journal-based recovery after a crash. A batch leaving memory must not become a hole in the projection merely because no worker had consumed it yet.

 Both table examples use a dense array indexed by bounded integer device IDs. Each entry holds two 64-bit integers. Updating an entry replaces those values without requiring a separate allocation for the update. Go's table: one slice of values. type Entry struct {
    Seq   uint64
    Value uint64
}

entries := make([]Entry, keyCount)
 Rust's table: one vector of values. #[derive(Clone, Copy, Default)]
struct Entry {
    seq: u64,
    value: u64,
}

let entries = vec![Entry::default(); key_count];
 Each logical entry contributes 16 bytes of field data. That excludes collection metadata and allocator overhead; actual memory use needs measuring on the target. Dense IDs make the layout reasonable. Sparse IDs could waste most of the table, while a hash table would introduce its own storage and hashing costs. In Go, keeping pointers out of this table reduces the collector's scanning work relative to a pointer-rich graph. To judge the remaining cost, inspect allocation rate and retained heap as well as collector CPU, including allocation assists. Pause time tells only part of that story. Rust has no tracing garbage collector in these examples. Destruction and deallocation still take time: dropping an owned structure or the last strong Arc can run cleanup on a latency-sensitive thread. The work depends on what is owned; a vector of these integer-only entries has much less to destroy than a graph of objects with custom cleanup.,  These examples avoid a long-lived graph of per-event objects. Their allocation pattern says little about a service with string keys or variable payloads.

 Here are four values, printed with their positions: enumerate() adds a position to each value. fn main() {
    for (index, value) in [1, 2, 3, 4]
        .into_iter()
        .enumerate()
    {
        println!("{index}: {value}");
    }
}
 The output starts at 0: 1 and ends at 3: 4. Somewhere, something remembers which value comes next and which index to attach to it. Rust's iteration protocol puts that responsibility in the iterator. Its required interface is small: The required part of Iterator, with its provided methods omitted. pub trait Iterator {
    type Item;

    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item>;
}
 A trait describes behavior a type can provide. Here, Item names the type of value it produces. next() asks for one value and returns either Some(value) or None. The &mut self parameter lets the iterator change its own state while answering. A for loop first asks IntoIterator to turn its input into an iterator, then repeatedly asks for the next value. For this simple array, we can sketch the loop like this: A sketch of the iteration behavior, not the complete language desugaring. let values = [10, 20, 30];
let mut iterator = IntoIterator::into_iter(values);

while let Some(value) = iterator.next() {
    println!("{value}");
}
 The loop stops at the first None. It doesn't need to know whether advancing means moving through an array, following a list, or reading from a file. The Iterator contract allows an implementation to return Some again after returning None. A for loop still stops at that first None. If you need later calls to keep returning None, fuse() supplies that behavior. Iterators implementing FusedIterator already promise it. This becomes relevant when we reach asynchronous streams: None is not a general-purpose instruction to wait and try again later.

 An adapter wraps an iterator and implements Iterator itself. That shared interface lets us connect adapters: each one can use another as its source, without knowing how that source obtains its values. To add a position, our wrapper needs to hold the source iterator and a counter: A small numbering adapter. The counter starts at zero when we construct it. struct Indexed<I> {
    source: I,
    index: usize,
}

impl<I: Iterator> Iterator for Indexed<I> {
    type Item = (usize, I::Item);

    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item> {
        let value = self.source.next()?;

        let index = self.index;
        self.index += 1;

        Some((index, value))
    }
}
 State between calls. The wrapper owns its source iterator and a counter. The source may itself borrow a collection; storing it here preserves that relationship. The same contract on both sides. I must implement Iterator, and Indexed<I> implements it too. Another adapter can use this wrapper as its source. Ask the source for one item. ? returns None if the source has no item on this call. The counter advances only after an item is obtained. I stands for whichever iterator we wrap. The bound I: Iterator lets us call its next() method, and I::Item names the type it produces. Our adapter returns a pair: a position and that source item. Because Indexed<I> also implements Iterator, it gets the trait's provided methods, including filter() and take(). The ? after self.source.next() returns None immediately if the source returned None. Otherwise, it extracts the value. We save the current index, increment the counter for next time, and return the pair. We can construct one with the counter set to zero and step through it ourselves: Each call advances the source and the counter held by the same object. let mut indexed = Indexed {
    source: [10, 20].into_iter(),
    index: 0,
};

assert_eq!(indexed.next(), Some((0, 10)));
assert_eq!(indexed.next(), Some((1, 20)));
assert_eq!(indexed.next(), None);
 The state persists in these fields between calls to next(). The method returns each time; the next call uses the values left by the previous one. Rust's standard-library Enumerate stores the same two pieces of state. It also implements shortcuts for operations such as nth(), fold(), and try_fold(). We will come back to why those matter. The example uses usize, just as Enumerate does. Its index can overflow on a sufficiently long sequence. The public enumerate() documentation says that enumeration can panic or produce an incorrect count when the counter overflows, with a panic guaranteed when overflow checks are enabled. This adapter is a model of ordinary enumeration, not an arbitrary-precision counter.

 The same structure can produce prefix sums. Keep a running total alongside the source, add each incoming value, and yield the new total. For 3, 1, 4, 2, the prefix sums are 3, 4, 8, 10. A custom prefix-sum adapter composes with the standard enumerate() and take() adapters. struct PrefixSums<I> {
    source: I,
    total: u64,
}

impl<I: Iterator<Item = u64>> Iterator for PrefixSums<I> {
    type Item = u64;

    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item> {
        self.total += self.source.next()?;
        Some(self.total)
    }
}

let sums: Vec<_> = PrefixSums {
    source: [3u64, 1, 4, 2].into_iter(),
    total: 0,
}
.enumerate()
.take(3)
.collect();

assert_eq!(sums, vec![(0, 3), (1, 4), (2, 8)]);
 Update the retained total. Each successful pull adds one source value. The same field holds 3, then 4, then 8 as the caller advances. Let the consumer stop the work. take(3) stops before asking for a fourth sum. The final input, 2, is not read by this chain. This time the bound is narrower: the source must produce u64 values so we can add them to the total. The adapters after it still work through Iterator. enumerate() numbers the sums, and take(3) stops the chain before the last input is read. Neither needs to know how PrefixSums maintains its total. As with the index counter, a reusable numeric adapter needs a policy for overflow. These small sums fit in u64. The standard library also provides scan(), which can express the same running total using stored state and a closure.

 Now suppose we want the first two even values and their positions in the original input: Two results retain their original positions; inspect() counts three source values. let values = [10, 11, 12, 13, 14];
let mut examined = 0;

let result: Vec<_> = values
    .into_iter()
    .inspect(|_| examined += 1)
    .enumerate()
    .filter(|(_, value)| value % 2 == 0)
    .take(2)
    .collect();

assert_eq!(examined, 3);
assert_eq!(result, vec![(0, 10), (2, 12)]);
 enumerate() produces pairs. filter() keeps pairs whose value is even. Its predicate borrows each item as &Self::Item to test it; an accepted item is then passed on by value. With borrowed source items, that test adds another layer of references. take(2) allows at most two items through, and collect() gathers them into a vector. It is easy to imagine a separate pass at every dot: number all five values, put the even ones somewhere, then copy the first two. These adapters can instead pass requests and values through the chain without building those intermediate collections. Think of asking the outer iterator for a match. It asks its source, which eventually reaches the array. The value 10 comes back with index 0, passes the filter, and becomes the first result. The second request takes more work. 11 gets index 1 but fails the filter. The filter asks again, receives (2, 12), and accepts it. Once take(2) has supplied two results, it stops asking the source for more.  <span class="figure-number">Figure 1: </span>Requests travel toward the source; accepted items return to the consumer. The second output needs two source reads. This is a semantic trace, not a literal optimized call graph. Open the full-size diagram The last two values aren't pulled through inspect(). Constructing the adapter chain didn't scan the input; consuming it caused the work. The final vector has storage of its own, but there is no vector between each adapter. There is a limit to this control. Asking for one output can require examining many inputs. A filter over an infinite sequence with no matches never returns a match. Calling take(2) limits the number of outputs; it does not promise two source reads, a time limit, or a bounded amount of work.

 Move enumerate() after the filter: Filtering first numbers the surviving values, not their original positions. let values = [10, 11, 12, 13, 14];

let result: Vec<_> = values
    .into_iter()
    .filter(|value| value % 2 == 0)
    .enumerate()
    .take(2)
    .collect();

assert_eq!(result, vec![(0, 10), (1, 12)]);
 Now 12 has index 1. Only the surviving values reach the counter. In a diagnostic tool, that could turn an original record position into a match number. Both are useful, but we need to choose which one we mean.

 Numbering works the same way whether the source produces owned values or references. The difference travels through Item: Expression Item type What the loop receives names.iter() &String A shared reference to each string names.iter_mut() &mut String An exclusive reference to each string names.into_iter() String Ownership of each string from this vector Here are all three with the same adapter: The first two loops borrow the vector; the last consumes it. let mut names = vec![
    String::from("Alice"),
    String::from("Bob"),
];

// Borrow the elements.
for (i, name) in names.iter().enumerate() {
    println!("{i}: {name}");
}

// Mutably borrow the elements.
for (_, name) in names.iter_mut().enumerate() {
    name.push('!');
}

// Transfer ownership of the elements.
for (i, name) in names.into_iter().enumerate() {
    println!("{i}: {name}");
}
 The last loop moves the strings out of the vector. It doesn't clone their contents, and we cannot use names afterward as though it still owned them. In the earlier loops, Rust checks that uses of the references respect their lifetimes and exclusive access requirements. enumerate() doesn't need separate code for each case. Its pair contains I::Item, whatever that happens to be. It adds a counter while leaving the ownership relationship intact. Ownership also determines cleanup. When we drop an adapter, its owned fields are dropped too, including the source iterator and any closure state. If a chain consumes a vector with into_iter(), dropping it also drops the vector iterator's unconsumed elements. A chain built with iter() only borrows the vector; dropping that chain leaves the elements in place. Early termination stops further pulls, but cleanup can still do work.

 Consider readings already loaded into a buffer. We want unverified readings above a latency threshold, with their original positions. We can offer that search without copying matching records into another collection: Returning a borrowed iterator lets the caller choose how many matches to inspect. #[derive(Debug)]
struct Reading {
    device_id: u64,
    latency_ms: u32,
    verified: bool,
}

fn suspicious<'a>(
    readings: &'a [Reading],
    threshold_ms: u32,
) -> impl Iterator<Item = (usize, &'a Reading)> + 'a {
    readings
        .iter()
        .enumerate()
        .filter(move |(_, reading)| {
            reading.latency_ms > threshold_ms
                && !reading.verified
        })
}
 Borrow from the caller's buffer. The yielded &'a Reading points into readings. Returning this iterator does not copy the records or extend the input's lifetime. Keep the threshold with the predicate. move captures threshold_ms by value. Since u32 is Copy, the closure keeps a copy; the source iterator still borrows the readings. Use this main() with Reading and suspicious() above. fn main() {
    let readings = [
        Reading {
            device_id: 101,
            latency_ms: 150,
            verified: true,
        },
        Reading {
            device_id: 102,
            latency_ms: 400,
            verified: false,
        },
        Reading {
            device_id: 103,
            latency_ms: 350,
            verified: false,
        },
    ];

    for (index, reading) in suspicious(&readings, 250).take(2) {
        println!("{index}: {reading:?}");
    }
}
 Choose how many matches to inspect. The first reading is verified and excluded. This call prints the entries at positions 1 and 2 without collecting them into a new vector. The function describes how to find a reading. Its caller can ask for two, find the first, or count every match. Returning an iterator keeps that choice open. The lifetime label 'a ties the references in the results to the borrowed input. It doesn't extend the life of the buffer or copy it somewhere safe. If a caller tries to keep using the search after the readings have gone away, the program fails to compile. The move keyword makes the closure capture threshold_ms by value. Because u32 implements Copy, the closure stores a copy of the threshold instead of borrowing the function's local variable. The readings remain borrowed through the source iterator.

 Using the same readings, we can retain two matches and drop the search before using them: Returned references remain usable after the iterator is dropped, while the readings stay valid. let mut matches = suspicious(&readings, 250);

let first = matches.next().expect("first matching reading");
let second = matches.next().expect("second matching reading");

drop(matches);

println!("{first:?}");
println!("{second:?}");
 Keep a result while advancing. first borrows a reading in the input buffer. It does not retain the temporary mutable borrow used to advance matches, so the next call is allowed. Drop the search, keep the references. The iterator owns its adapter state, but only borrows the readings. Destroying that state does not destroy the caller's buffer. Each call briefly borrows the iterator mutably so it can advance. The returned references borrow readings, not the iterator object. Holding the first result doesn't keep that call's mutable borrow of the iterator open, so we can ask for another. Dropping the iterator leaves both references usable as long as the underlying readings remain valid. The signature separates these lifetimes. Item is fixed for the iterator type; it isn't parameterized by the temporary borrow of each next() call. In this search it contains &'a Reading, with 'a tied to the input slice. A mutable iterator must also make it safe to retain several results, for example by yielding references to distinct elements. That is a limit on what ordinary Iterator can express. It cannot generally lend out a reference into its own scratch buffer and then overwrite that buffer on the next call while the reference is still in use. Such an API needs a different ownership arrangement, or a lending-style contract that ties each result to the call's borrow. We don't need that here: the caller already owns the buffer. impl Iterator hides a concrete return type that would be awkward to write out. The compiler still knows that type, including the closure and the wrapped iterators. Returning it this way does not introduce a boxed object or a virtual method call.

 The trait tells us what an iterator can do. Rust lets us choose how a call reaches the implementation. Here are two functions asking for the same thing: A concrete generic parameter. fn next_static<I: Iterator<Item = u64>>(
    source: &mut I,
) -> Option<u64> {
    source.next()
}
 A borrowed trait object. fn next_dynamic(
    source: &mut dyn Iterator<Item = u64>,
) -> Option<u64> {
    source.next()
}
 With next_static, the compiler knows the concrete I and can specialize the function for it. This is static dispatch. With next_dynamic, the function accepts a trait object. Its reference carries a pointer to the iterator state and vtable metadata used to find the method at runtime. This is dynamic dispatch. The iterator still holds its state in either case. Dispatch changes how we reach the method, not who drives the sequence or whether it is lazy. Notice that next_dynamic borrows an iterator. It doesn't allocate one. We can pass a reference to an iterator already stored in a local variable. Heap allocation and dynamic dispatch are separate choices. For our telemetry query, each adapter and closure has a concrete type. suspicious() returns the resulting chain through impl Iterator, which hides its name without erasing that type. The compiler keeps the information needed to specialize calls and consider inlining them. We haven't needed a runtime choice of implementation between stages. A concrete wrapper can still contain a dynamically dispatched iterator. Adding take() around a trait object doesn't make calls to that underlying source static. Suppose a query can return every value or only even ones, depending on a setting read at runtime: Two concrete iterator types share one boxed return type, while the input remains borrowed. fn choose_values<'a>(
    values: &'a [u64],
    only_even: bool,
) -> Box<dyn Iterator<Item = u64> + 'a> {
    if only_even {
        Box::new(values.iter().copied().filter(|x| x % 2 == 0))
    } else {
        Box::new(values.iter().copied())
    }
}
 The branches construct different types: one includes a filter and the other doesn't. Box<dyn Iterator<Item = u64> + 'a> gives them a common return type. The box owns the iterator state; the slice remains borrowed. The lifetime bound keeps that borrow valid, and Item = u64 tells callers what either implementation produces. A plain impl Iterator return cannot hide a different concrete type in each branch. For this particular condition we could instead use one filter with a configurable predicate. An enum could also represent a fixed set of alternatives. A trait object becomes useful when we want a common interface without making that set part of its type. Here the box supplies owned heap storage for the iterator, not a newly allocated record on every call. The earlier &mut dyn Iterator example supplies dynamic dispatch without that storage choice. Both retain the usual ownership and borrowing checks. Static specialization can increase generated code size and compilation work when many concrete types use the same generic code. Dynamic dispatch can let a consumer share one implementation across those types. An indirect call may make inlining harder, although the optimizer can sometimes recover the target. If this trade-off matters to the application, it needs measurement.,  Traits must satisfy Rust's dyn compatibility rules to support trait objects. Iterator does, once we specify its associated Item type. Its next() method can be called through the vtable. Many generic adapter and consumer methods instead require Self: Sized, which excludes those methods from the dynamically callable interface. That doesn't prevent further composition. A sized wrapper such as Box<dyn Iterator<Item = u64>> or &mut dyn Iterator<Item = u64> itself implements Iterator. We can add adapters around it. The outer adapter has a concrete type, while requests reaching the erased source still use its dynamic interface. A generic wrapper doesn't automatically make every call inside it static. For a visual tour of trait-object pointers, see Sofía Belén López Vicens's Visualizing Rust's VTables: How dyn Trait Works in Memory. Exact byte layouts and vtable slot offsets should not be treated as a stable Rust ABI.

 An iterator chain can compile to code similar to a hand-written loop. Rust specializes generic code for concrete types through monomorphization. Inlining can then bring operations from different adapters together so the optimizer can work across them. For example, these functions calculate the same sum. The wrapping operations make overflow behavior explicit: A loop that squares even values and adds them with wrapping arithmetic. pub fn manual(input: &[u64]) -> u64 {
    let mut sum = 0u64;

    for &x in input {
        if x % 2 == 0 {
            sum = sum.wrapping_add(x.wrapping_mul(x));
        }
    }

    sum
}
 The same calculation expressed as an iterator chain. pub fn adapted(input: &[u64]) -> u64 {
    input
        .iter()
        .copied()
        .filter(|x| x % 2 == 0)
        .map(|x| x.wrapping_mul(x))
        .fold(0u64, |sum, x| sum.wrapping_add(x))
}
 Inlining and loop optimization can make the generated code resemble the handwritten version. Whether they do so for a particular program depends on the compiler, target, optimization settings, and surrounding code. We would have to inspect that output and benchmark representative inputs to make a performance claim. There is another wrinkle in our request-by-request picture. The standard library can implement a consumer such as fold() by passing the whole accumulation through the adapters. Its Enumerate implementation has specialized fold() and try_fold() methods, so consuming a chain need not literally become a tower of next() calls. The pull model explains the sequence's behavior; optimized bulk operations can implement it differently. Some costs are visible without a benchmark. Our simple adapter chain doesn't require an intermediate collection at every step. It uses concrete types instead of a required virtual call at every boundary. Neither fact makes the predicate free, guarantees vectorization, or eliminates allocation in user-supplied code. In Fast Writes, Fresh Reads, moving work into a background thread introduced replay and coordination costs. Here, calling filter() doesn't start a worker at all. The caller runs the predicate as it consumes the iterator. A fluent chain of methods doesn't, by itself, tell us where computation runs.

 The telemetry example starts with a slice already in memory. A live connection has another possibility: there is no new reading yet, but more may arrive. An ordinary iterator can block inside next() until data arrives. Its interface doesn't say whether a call will return quickly. What it lacks is a way to return "not ready" together with an asynchronous wakeup arrangement. The Stream trait from futures-core adds that distinction: The poll_next signature from futures-core::Stream, with surrounding declarations omitted. fn poll_next(
    self: Pin<&mut Self>,
    cx: &mut Context<'_>,
) -> Poll<Option<Self::Item>>;
 Result Meaning Ready(Some(value)) A value is available Ready(None) The stream has ended Pending No value yet; arrange a wakeup when progress may be possible Context gives the implementation access to a waker. Pin supports implementations whose state must stay at a stable location while pinned. Those requirements serve asynchronous execution, beyond the in-memory loop we started with. Rust's async computations use the Future protocol. The compiler represents their progress in a generated state machine, retaining what is needed across an await.,  A coroutine lets a function suspend and later resume from where it stopped. A synchronous generator uses that ability to yield values one at a time, with its consumer requesting each resumption. When producing a value involves nested loops and several yield points, that can be easier to follow than a hand-written state machine. Our numbering adapter needed only a source and a counter; more complicated control flow can justify the extra mechanism. There is also a boundary between local demand and system backpressure. A caller can stop pulling this iterator while a device, broker, or network buffer keeps receiving data. The capacity, loss, and admission policies from Backpressure in a Telemetry Pipeline still need to exist outside the iterator. A take() at the end of a chain doesn't bound every queue upstream.

 C++ ranges let us build a similar lazy pipeline, so they give us a way to ask which parts of the design are specific to Rust. C++23 provides enumeration directly: C++23's standard enumeration view needs no custom coroutine generator. #include <array>
#include <iostream>
#include <ranges>

int main() {
    std::array values{1, 2, 3, 4};

    for (auto [index, value] : std::views::enumerate(values)) {
        std::cout << index << ": " << value << '\n';
    }
}
 C++20 ranges already offered lazy composition; C++23 added this standard numbering adapter. Comparing a small Rust enumerate() call with the plumbing of a hand-built C++ coroutine generator would measure two different implementation choices. C++ ranges use iterator and sentinel interfaces, concepts, and views. A sentinel marks the end condition; it need not have the same type as the iterator. Those interfaces support lazy computation and compiler optimization too. Rust doesn't have exclusive access to that technique. Both languages support references, moves, and deterministic destruction. Rust's borrowing rules add compiler checks for reference lifetimes and exclusive access across the composition. C++ APIs can express ownership and borrowing relationships, but ordinary references don't generally carry the same static checks. The borrowing example is where I find the comparison most useful: who must establish that these records are still valid when a result is used?

 Once the booking operation has a home, I want its entry point to look familiar. I use this shape for application handlers: A common contract for application work, plus a function adapter used by wrappers and tests. type Handler[I, O any] interface {
    Handle(context.Context, I) (O, error)
}

type HandlerFunc[I, O any] func(context.Context, I) (O, error)

func (f HandlerFunc[I, O]) Handle(
    ctx context.Context, input I,
) (O, error) {
    return f(ctx, input)
}

type Void struct{}
 The input describes one operation. The result has a concrete type, and failures use an error. A command with no result payload uses Void. A query uses its result type. That keeps logging and instrumentation wrappers independent of any particular booking request. A function with the same signature could do this work. HandlerFunc lets such a function participate in the same composition. I use the interface for handlers that hold dependencies, with a consistent Handle method for their wrappers. Across several operations, that saves me from writing a separate wrapper API for each one. For a single short function, I would often keep the function. I keep command and query intent visible in the application packages. A query returning bookings and a command recording one make different promises, even though both fit the handler interface. This separation is useful without separate read databases or event sourcing. The common signature does not decide whether an operation is safe to repeat. A command may provide idempotency. A query may observe different data on the next attempt or consume enough resources that retrying it makes an outage worse. Those are properties of the operation and its dependencies. Handler gives the wrappers a common invocation mechanism. The concrete bookDeliveryHandler coordinates the booking use case, delegating the expiry decision to the domain and recording the booking through its repository port: Booking orchestration. The constructor supplies the repository and a time source; the domain receives a time value. type BookDelivery struct {
    ID        string
    HoldUntil time.Time
}

type bookDeliveryHandler struct {
    repository DeliveryBookingRepository
    now        func() time.Time
}

func NewBookDeliveryHandler(
    repository DeliveryBookingRepository,
    now func() time.Time,
) decorator.Handler[BookDelivery, decorator.Void] {
    return bookDeliveryHandler{repository: repository, now: now}
}

func (h bookDeliveryHandler) Handle(
    ctx context.Context, cmd BookDelivery,
) (decorator.Void, error) {
    b, err := booking.NewDeliveryBooking(
        cmd.ID, cmd.HoldUntil, h.now(),
    )
    if err != nil {
        return decorator.Void{}, err
    }
    if err := h.repository.RecordDeliveryBooking(ctx, b); err != nil {
        return decorator.Void{}, fmt.Errorf("record booking: %w", err)
    }
    return decorator.Void{}, nil
}
 The HTTP adapter can decode its generated request type into BookDelivery and map the returned error to an HTTP response. A broker adapter can construct the same command from an event. Neither needs to reimplement the hold rule. The constructor returns decorator.Handler to keep construction and decoration on the same public contract. Returning the concrete handler would also work; it satisfies the interface when passed to ChainDecorators. Go's usual guidance favors returning concrete types. Here, I accept hiding any additional methods from callers. If they need more behavior, I reconsider that choice instead of adding type assertions around it.

 For the example, a booking is valid only while its hold has not expired. I want the boundary case to be explicit: at the deadline itself, the hold is expired. The illustrative domain model. Time comes from the caller, so the expiry rule is deterministic for a given input. var ErrInvalidID = errors.New("booking ID is required")
var ErrHoldExpired = errors.New("booking hold expired")

type DeliveryBooking struct {
    id        string
    holdUntil time.Time
}

func NewDeliveryBooking(
    id string, holdUntil, now time.Time,
) (DeliveryBooking, error) {
    if id == "" {
        return DeliveryBooking{}, ErrInvalidID
    }
    if !now.Before(holdUntil) {
        return DeliveryBooking{}, ErrHoldExpired
    }
    return DeliveryBooking{id: id, holdUntil: holdUntil}, nil
}

func (b DeliveryBooking) ID() string { return b.id }
func (b DeliveryBooking) HoldUntil() time.Time { return b.holdUntil }
 The application obtains time through its injected function. The composition root can supply time.Now; a test can supply a fixed instant. The domain imports time for its value type and comparisons, but never asks the environment what time it is. That lets a test choose an instant just before the deadline, the exact deadline, and an instant afterward. There is no sleep and no dependence on how quickly a test machine happens to run. There is still a business decision to make about time. The handler above checks the hold when it handles the request. If the intended rule is based on when an event was accepted elsewhere, the input and trust assumptions need to express that instead. Injecting a clock makes the choice testable; it does not choose the correct policy.

 A handler that records a booking still needs logging and metrics. Putting that work in every handler body makes ordinary changes noisy. Because the entry point has a consistent type, I can wrap it: A decorator takes a handler and returns a handler. Applying the list in reverse makes the first listed decorator outermost. type Decorator[I, O any] func(Handler[I, O]) Handler[I, O]

func ChainDecorators[I, O any](
    handler Handler[I, O], decorators ...Decorator[I, O],
) Handler[I, O] {
    for i := len(decorators) - 1; i >= 0; i-- {
        handler = decorators[i](handler)
    }
    return handler
}
 At construction, I choose the wrappers and their order: Application construction. The wrapper implementations are omitted here; each preserves the same Handle contract. bookDelivery := decorator.ChainDecorators(
    command.NewBookDeliveryHandler(repository, time.Now),
    decorator.WithLogging[command.BookDelivery, decorator.Void](logger),
    decorator.WithMetrics[command.BookDelivery, decorator.Void](observeBooking),
)
 A request enters logging, then metrics, then the booking handler. Results return through those wrappers in the opposite order. The operation can be tested with no wrappers, and the composition can be tested separately when order matters.  <span class="figure-number">Figure 3: </span>The talk adds retry inside logging and metrics, so those outer wrappers observe the overall operation. The retry wrapper still requires an established repeat contract. Suppose I later add a retry wrapper. Placing it inside the metrics wrapper makes that metric describe one overall invocation, including retries. Placing metrics inside retry makes it describe individual attempts. Either can be useful, but they answer different questions. If I need both, I instrument both explicitly. The booking constructor leaves retry out until I've decided how to handle a repeated request, including one that arrives after the hold has expired.

 The repository interface gives the booking test a small substitution point. A function type is enough to implement it: A function fake used by application tests. type repositoryFunc func(
    context.Context, booking.DeliveryBooking,
) error

func (f repositoryFunc) RecordDeliveryBooking(
    ctx context.Context, b booking.DeliveryBooking,
) error {
    return f(ctx, b)
}
 The test can choose a storage error without starting MongoDB: The handler must preserve a storage failure for the caller to classify. func TestBookDeliveryPreservesStorageFailure(t *testing.T) {
    unavailable := errors.New("storage unavailable")
    now := time.Date(2026, 10, 31, 10, 0, 0, 0, time.UTC)
    repository := repositoryFunc(func(
        context.Context, booking.DeliveryBooking,
    ) error {
        return unavailable
    })
    handler := command.NewBookDeliveryHandler(
        repository, func() time.Time { return now },
    )

    _, err := handler.Handle(context.Background(), command.BookDelivery{
        ID: "booking-42", HoldUntil: now.Add(time.Minute),
    })
    if !errors.Is(err, unavailable) {
        t.Fatalf("got %v, want storage failure", err)
    }
}
 I would also check that an expired hold never reaches the repository, and that a valid command records the expected domain value. These tests run real domain and application code. Only the persistence dependency is replaced. They say nothing about whether the MongoDB adapter maps the ID to the correct field. For that, I need the real adapter and a real database. I use different test scopes to exercise different failure modes:  Scope What runs for real Booking failure it can expose Domain unit Booking values and rules A hold is accepted at its expiry instant Use case Domain and handler; function fake at the repository port A storage failure is swallowed or invalid input reaches persistence Adapter integration One adapter and its backing dependency The stored representation cannot be read back correctly Component, in process Service composition with selected dependencies substituted Wiring supplies the wrong implementation or wrappers run in the wrong order Component, out of process Built service with provisioned dependencies Configuration, startup, or the external request contract is wrong End to end Collaborating services A neighboring consumer interprets the booking differently These are not six suites I generate for every handler. I choose the cheapest scope that actually exercises the risk. Index initialization can be checked by a focused integration test if that test runs the initializer; it does not inherently require launching the entire service.  <span class="figure-number">Figure 4: </span>The solid areas show real code or dependencies; dashed areas show substitutes. Here, ports names the transport-adapter packages, corresponding to adapters/http in the example layout. The core interfaces remain application-owned. Testcontainers lets the test process provision dependencies such as MongoDB or a broker. The harness still needs isolated state and reliable cleanup. Running a real database does not make tests parallel-safe if they all write to the same collection with the same identities. As the scope widens, the booking behavior can stay recognizable. I can exercise a repository contract against a fake and a real implementation, then run a relevant booking scenario through the built service. That gives the fake something concrete to stay compatible with. The fast test remains useful without pretending it covers the driver or deployment packaging. In CI/CD Across Team Boundaries, I discuss the next boundary: a change that only becomes meaningful when several teams' services interact. The local service tests establish their own claims; a cross-service scenario checks the collaboration those tests cannot see.

A Rust learning exercise with nested Olympic JSON: read country fields, count medals and rank results, with a worked example to follow. As I was learning Rust, I wanted a practical project to build some syntax muscle memory. Loading JSON seemed like a useful place to start, so I chose a nested dataset from the Tokyo Olympics and tried to turn it into a medal table. We’ll follow the steps from reading the nested fields to counting medals for each country and ranking the results. A country lookup that needs a fallback gives us one detail to work through along the way. The code Use a medal type to select its counter What I learned Next steps The exercise uses serde_json to read athletics data from the Olympic Games Tokyo 2020. It ranks countries by gold medals, then silver, then bronze, and prints the first five. Print the top five countries and the medals they won. United States of America MedalCount { g: 7, s: 12, b: 7 }
Italy MedalCount { g: 5, s: 0, b: 0 }
Kenya MedalCount { g: 4, s: 4, b: 2 }
Poland MedalCount { g: 4, s: 2, b: 3 }
Jamaica MedalCount { g: 4, s: 1, b: 4 }
 I chose an excerpt of the athletics data set because it has more to work through than a small, uniform example. The data is deeply nested, and the countryObject field needs closer inspection. <span class="figure-number">Figure 1: </span>Structure of our JSON data The expanded path runs through pageProps, gameDiscipline, events, awards, and participant to countryObject. Open the full-size JSON tree To produce the medal table, we need to read medalType and the country information from each award, as shown above. A detail in the data countryObject is not always an object. The lookup tests is_object() before choosing the field and otherwise falls back to country. See the country lookup

 Serde maps medal values to Class. For counting, I want each variant to select the corresponding counter in MedalCount, so the accumulation loop can stay short. Rust’s Index trait supplies read access to a selected counter; IndexMut supplies mutable access. The implementations below map each Class variant to the corresponding g, s or b field.  impl Index<Class> for MedalCount {
    type Output = usize;

    fn index(&self, class: Class) -> &Self::Output {
        match class {
            Class::Gold => &self.g,
            Class::Silver => &self.s,
            Class::Bronze => &self.b,
        }
    }
}

impl IndexMut<Class> for MedalCount {
    fn index_mut(&mut self, class: Class) -> &mut Self::Output {
        match class {
            Class::Gold => &mut self.g,
            Class::Silver => &mut self.s,
            Class::Bronze => &mut self.b,
        }
    }
}
 As far as I know, serde_json does not support mapping of nested data to struct via some form of dot notation. Luckily, if data is not mapped to a predefined struct than the JSON data can still be deserialized into a Value, which then allows us to get the nested data we want. Follow the three operations in this listing: Extract country names, count medals, and rank the results. fn get_class_country_tuple<'a>(values: &'a Vec<Value>) -> Vec<(Class, String)> {
    let get_country = |participant: &'a serde_json::Value| -> &'a serde_json::Value {
        let mut country_key = "countryObject";
        if !participant[country_key].is_object() {
            country_key = "country";
        }

        &participant[country_key]["name"]
    };

    let to_tuple = |v: &'a Value| {
        let class = serde_json::from_value::<Class>(v["medalType"].to_owned()).unwrap();
        let country = get_country(&v["participant"]).as_str().unwrap().to_string();
        (class, country)
    };

    values.iter().map(to_tuple).collect()
}

// returns a projection on the medals per country data
pub fn get_medals_per_country(&self) -> Result<Projection, Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let to_medal_country_tuple =
        |v: &serde_json::Value| get_class_country_tuple(v["awards"].as_array().unwrap());

    let group_by_country = |mut acc: HashMap<_, _>, x| {
        let (class, country): (Class, String) = x;

        // remember the IndexMut trait, we used it here for indexing
        // If there is no entry for a country yet, we create one and
        // initialize the medal count to g: 0, s: 0, b: 0.
        // The fields will be incremented by one based on the indexing
        // operations defined earlier.
        // So, the value "Gold" will be deserialized to the enum variant
        // Class::Gold which will increment the field `g` of teh struct
        // MedalCount.
        acc.entry(country).or_insert(MedalCount::default())[class] += 1;
        acc
    };

    let events = self.0.json["pageProps"]["gameDiscipline"]["events"]
        .as_array()
        .unwrap();

    let mut medals_per_country = events
        .into_iter()
        .flat_map(to_medal_country_tuple)
        .fold(HashMap::new(), group_by_country)
        .into_iter()
        .collect::<Vec<(_, _)>>();

    medals_per_country.sort_by(|a, b| b.1.cmp(&a.1));

    Ok(Projection(medals_per_country))
}
 Extract the country name. The lookup uses countryObject when it is an object, otherwise country. Accumulate the medals. Each country gets a counter; IndexMut selects the gold, silver, or bronze field to increment. Rank the results. The comparison orders by gold first, then silver, then bronze—not by the total medal count.  #[derive(Eq, PartialEq)]
struct Projection(Vec<(String, MedalCount)>);

impl Projection {
    fn empty() -> Self {
        Self(vec![])
    }

    fn take(self, n: usize) -> Self {
        Self(self.0.into_iter().take(n).collect())
    }

    fn get(&self) -> &Vec<(String, MedalCount)> {
        &self.0
    }
}
 Having all the components in place, we can finally implement the logic we wanted:  fn load_data() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let url =
        "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sekaiser/rust-sample-parse-json/main/athletics.json";
    let db = AthleticsDb::from_url(Url::from_str(url)?)?;

    let mut last_top5 = Projection::empty();
    loop {
        let medals_per_country = db.get_medals_per_country()?;
        let top5 = medals_per_country.take(5);

        if top5 != last_top5 {
            for e in top5.get() {
                println!("{} {:?}", e.0, e.1);
            }

            last_top5 = top5;
        }
        thread::sleep(time::Duration::from_secs(2));
    }
}
